  mistake in misidentifying brian but you  have to question whether or not roberto  laundry was being intentionally  deceptive i still believe that they  could be charged with accessory after  the fact you just have to show they knew  about a crime and that they aided in the  escape and i think they can be charged  with obstruction of justice if you think  about it jesse brian laundry came home  on september 1st they went camping  together as a family on september 6th  and 7th they have to know what happened  so i definitely think they're holding  back information and i do think charges  can be made against them i don't know  driving that mustang it's a little  curious to think about and jeannette  thank you so much for reporting on this  appreciate as always coming up on law  and crime daily a victim of serial  killer john wayne gacy is positively  identified how investigators solved the  mystery of a john doe victim more than  40 years after his murder  [Music]  [Music]  and welcome back more than four decades  after his murder investigative work and  dna testing helped identify an unknown  john wayne gacy victim as missing man  francis wayne alexander  wayne came here in 75  and was only in here for a short period  of time  he had the misfortune of both living in  the area where john waste wayne gacy did  most of his killing where he target most  of his victims  he also had the misfortune of also  operating an area where john gasey  targeted specific people in specific  groups as well it was like the typical  painstaking work of finding out when  wayne was last seen  tax records  parking tickets that we were able to put  this all together combining with the dna  alexander's remains were found in the  crawl space under gacy's chicago area  home back in 1978 the serial killer was  charged with 33 murders he was dubbed  the so-called killer clown as he often  dressed as a clown and performed at  children's hospitals or events gacy was  sentenced to death and executed in 1994.  on monday the cook county sheriff  announced alexander is a victim citing  police work and scientific research from  the dna doe project dna from alexander's  tooth was sent to three different states  to find a genealogical match the cook  county sheriff says that five of gacy's  victims still remain unidentified but  they say officials say that they will  continue their investigations to  determine who the remaining unidentified  victims are  so suanne how does a discovery like this  tell us more about gacy and of course  his crime spree  it tells you that we don't really know  the extent of  everything he did even post his death  and i think the big big  word of the day for this family is  definitely closure they finally know  what happened to their family member  after so long and that to me is the most  important part of the story  along with how much  police work and investigation goes into  it and that advancements in tech  technology are allowing for cold cases  to be solved so many years later yeah  and that's a great point and terry you  know how important are these kind of dna  genealogy databases and sites when it  comes to identifying unknown victims  it's critically important and it's not  just for victims it's actually for those  people who commit the crimes you  remember in the golden state killer case  the authorities followed him and they  found some of his dna in a discarded  item and they did the same thing they  traced back genealogy and they did find  his  family members and ultimately they did  that police work that we talked about  and it took them back to the killer the  same thing happens with the victims here  they find something and they trace it  back to a family member and that is how  they ultimately are able to find and  swear is right it is the closure that  these family members are getting and  sometimes the family members have to be  really aggressive as far as chasing this  down and following up with authorities  to make sure they figure out what  happened to their family member yeah and  it's great that law enforcement never  gave up and maybe we'll have an end to  cold cases moving forward moving on to  news out of new york where eric garner's  family is pushing back with a judicial  inquiry seven years after he died at the  hands of nypd officers you might recall  that in 2014 garner was approached by  police officers who say he was selling  cigarettes without tax stamps and now  former police officer daniel pantaleo  attempted to arrest garner who pulled  his arms away pantaleo then placed his  arm around garner's neck and wrestled  him to the ground as multiple officers  pinned him down garner repeated the  words i can't breathe 11 times while  face down on the sidewalk garner lost  consciousness and was later pronounced  dead hours later at the hospital a  ground a grand jury decided to not  indict pantaleo and an out of court  settlement was reached with the city of  new york and the garner family pantaleo  was later fired in 2019. that was five  years after the incident on monday  garner's mother and other  petitioners began their judicial inquiry  it's actually a rarely used legal  process that allows members of the  public to investigate alleged neglect of  duty by public officials in new york  city judge erica edwards stated that she  will not charge anyone at the end of the  inquiry and that the questioning will  focus on what really happened leading up  to garner's death  when we come back testimony begins in  florida from markeith lloyd's second  murder trial you're going to hear from  the defendant who addresses the court  about his arrest and what he says is  important in this case stay with us  welcome back everybody after nearly 12  days of jury selection jurors are seated  and testimony is underway in the second  murder trial of markeith loyd lloyd is  being tried for the murder of lieutenant  debra clayton who he shot outside of a  walmart while she was pursuing him in  january 2017. at the time lloyd was  running from from authorities after  killing his pregnant ex-girlfriend  shaudai dixon you might recall that in  2019 lloyd was found guilty of dixon's  murder now he originally faced the death  penalty but was later sentenced to life  with no parole if lloyd is convicted in  this murder case he will face the death  penalty again opening statements  officially began on monday after jury  selection concluded lloyd previously  requested that he represent himself in  this case having little faith in his  defense team and following their opening  statements on monday he expressed those  concerns once again  and then an opening he left out  an important part of my defense which  everyone should know what these jurors  should know even if even miss clayton's  family should know how to turn myself in  two times before walmart they don't want  to talk about that they don't want the  jury to know  walmart never had to happen  these people know these people know i  these people know  that i believe the police were trying to  kill me and they told no one  they endangered the devil me and  everyone else but they told no way and  they trying to keep the jury and the  people from knowing this  and this judge is working with them  and walmart never had to have i tried to  turn myself in two times before walmart  i know i would never kill your queen  like that never  okay so suanne if you have a client  who's unhappy with your representation  he gets convicted and sentenced does  that mean that that client is  automatically entitled to a new trial  no  the client would have to file a motion  and  the  the conduct of the attorney would have  to rise to a certain level in order for  him to be entitled to a new trial or or  the standard of the attorney's  uh  representation would have to be very low  in order for him to be granted a new  trial i think you know he's in a unique  situation where he already has a life  sentence pending so if he chooses to  represent himself you know he's he's  kind of you know not to make light of it  but he's kind of playing with house  money he's already facing a life  sentence well here's the thing terry  right if markeith lloyd's convicted of  murder the you know for the death of uh  officer clayton do you think he could  escape the death sentence once again he  testified in his first trial maybe he'll  testify again you know what jesse he  could escape the death sentence again  and i think he will testify on this  stand but i think this time it's going  to be different the prosecution's  opening was amazing they listed the fact  that they have multiple witnesses who  identified him at this walmart we know  he shot clayton four different times he  also shot at another officer who was  tracking him down he stopped a bystander  and took his car this individual is  ruthless and tried very hard to get away  so i think the other issue here is  the prosecution mentioned in their  opening that he wasn't diagnosed with  mental illness until after he was on  trial i think if the jury looks at all  of that they could very well sentence  him to death maybe all right wait and  see particularly if he takes the stand  suanne terry thank you so much everyone  out there thanks for joining us here on  long crime daily we'll see you next time  as we discuss justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

